ion reports of fungi on plants provide a comprehensive account of the host range and geographic distribution of fungi on plants throughout the world. Data are continuously added to the databases from herbarium specimens and newly published fungus-host distributions and disease reports. Additional databases contain taxonomic literature references and accurate scientific names of plant pathogenic fungi.","logo":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/collicon/bpi.jpg","url":"https://fungi.ars.usda.gov","emllink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=24","archivelink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/dwca/bpi_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"Lisa.Castlebury@USDA.GOV"},{"last_name":"Lisa A. Castlebury","email":"Lisa.Castlebury@USDA.GOV"},{"last_name":"Lisa A. Castlebury","email":"Lisa.Castlebury@USDA.GOV"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"e9c875f0-d9c9-426b-bdc8-0999eb1b6deb","totalCount":962117,"hitRatio":0.0000072756224035122545},{"uuid":"36d35b23-113e-4633-90ec-19d265a3b5f6","itemCount":5,"name":"The New York Botanical Garden Herbarium (NY)","description":"The William and Lynda Steere Herbarium of The New York Botanical Garden holds a collection of more than seven million preserved specimens. All plant groups – flowering plants, conifers, ferns, mosses, liverworts, and algae, as well as fungi and lichens - are represented in the collection, which is particularly strong in specimens from the Western Hemisphere. Digitization of all groups is ongoing, with ±4.7 million specimens barcoded and ±3.8 million imaged.","logo":"https://sweetgum.nybg.org/science/wp-content/themes/nybgscience/images/logo23.svg","url":"https://sweetgum.nybg.org/science/vh/","emllink":"https://sweetgum.nybg.org:8443/ipt/eml.do?r=occurrences","archivelink":"https://sweetgum.nybg.org:8443/ipt/archive.do?r=occurrences","contacts":[{"first_name":"Joel","last_name":"Ramirez","role":"Web Developer for Biodiversity Information Management","email":"jramirez@nybg.org"},{"first_name":"Kimberly","last_name":"Watson","role":"Assistant Director of Biodiversity Information Management","email":"kwatson@nybg.org"},{"first_name":"Leanna","last_name":"Feder","role":"Herbarium Digital Asset Manager","email":"lfeder@nybg.org"},{"first_name":"Elizabeth","last_name":"Gjieli","role":"Geographical Information Manager","email":"egjieli@nybg.org"},{"first_name":"Emily","last_name":"Sessa","role":"Director of the Herbarium","email":"esessa@nybg.org"},{"first_name":"Kimberly","last_name":"Watson","email":"kwatson@nybg.org"},{"first_name":"Kimberly","last_name":"Watson","role":"Assistant Director of Biodiversity Information Management","email":"kwatson@nybg.org"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"e84b1163-233d-4e46-9eb8-aa2d0d3ff7d8","totalCount":4808642,"hitRatio":0.0000010397946031332755},{"uuid":"8f3b62fb-56ec-49e8-9f8f-bb257348291f","itemCount":2,"name":"University of Nebraska State Museum, C.E. Bessey Herbarium - Fungi","description":"The Bessey Herbarium was founded in 1874, making it among the oldest in the Great Plains states of Colorado, Kansas, Montana, North Dakota, and Nebraska. The collection has more than 310,000 specimens, placing it among the largest in the Great Plains. The largest parts of the collection are, in descending order, from Nebraska, the Great Plains, the Rocky Mountains, other parts of North America, and Europe. It contains important collections by such scientifically notable Nebraskans as Charles Bessey, Ernst Bessey, Frederic Clements, Walter Kiener, Per Rydberg, Raymond Pool, Jared G. Smith; by other Nebraskans who later became prominent in other fields, such as Roscoe Pound (Law), Louise Pound (Literature), Willa Cather (Literature), Melvin Gilmore (Ethnobotany), Lawrence Bruner (Entomology) and Henry Baldwin Ward (Parasitology); and by many prominent scientists from outside the state.","logo":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/collicon/neb.gif","url":"https://museum.unl.edu/collections/botany/index.html","emllink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=50","archivelink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/dwca/neb_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"twidhelm2@unl.edu"},{"first_name":"Todd","last_name":"Widhelm","role":"Curator, Bessey Herbarium","email":"twidhelm2@unl.edu"},{"first_name":"Susan","last_name":"Weller","role":"Director, Morrill Hall","email":"susan.weller@unl.edu"},{"first_name":"Todd","last_name":"Widhelm","role":"Curator, Bessey Herbarium","email":"twidhelm2@unl.edu"},{"first_name":"Susan","last_name":"Weller","role":"Director, Morrill Hall","email":"susan.weller@unl.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC4 BY-NC","publisher":"e9c875f0-d9c9-426b-bdc8-0999eb1b6deb","totalCount":52784,"hitRatio":0.000037890269778720825},{"uuid":"0583609b-202f-40d0-8021-4c019635d4c9","itemCount":1,"name":"University of Michigan Herbarium","description":"The University of Michigan Herbarium is home to some of the finest botanical collections in the world. The 1.7 million specimens of vascular plants, algae, bryophytes, fungi, and lichens combined with the expertise of the faculty-curators, students, and staff provide a world class facility for teaching and research in systematic biology and biodiversity studies.  The organismal and genetic resource collections such as those in the Herbarium provide the best tangible record we have of life on Earth and constitute a crucial resource for use in research and education benefiting science, society, and the university.  Working collaboratively within the highly regarded Department of Ecology & Evolutionary Biology, our goal is to make UM a leading center for training and research in studies of the history, the change mechanisms, and the conservation of Earth’s diverse life forms.","url":"https://lsa.umich.edu/herbarium","emllink":"https://ipt.lsa.umich.edu/eml.do?r=umherb","archivelink":"https://ipt.lsa.umich.edu/archive.do?r=umherb","contacts":[{"last_name":"UM Herbarium Data Group","role":"Group","email":"umherb-data@umich.edu"},{"first_name":"LSA","last_name":"IPT Admin","role":"System Administrators","email":"lsa-it-ipt-admins@umich.edu"},{"first_name":"LSA","last_name":"IPT Admin","email":"lsa-it-ipt-admins@umich.edu"},{"last_name":"UM Herbarium Data Group","role":"Group","email":"umherb-data@umich.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC4 BY-NC","publisher":"8c493255-5867-485d-aaea-19a6e10c2ca5","totalCount":1251389,"hitRatio":7.991120267159133e-7},{"uuid":"37d4d085-d8be-4826-9bc4-c6a36557fa70","itemCount":1,"name":"Oregon State University Herbarium","description":"The Oregon State University Herbarium houses approximately 405,000 vascular plant, bryophyte, algal, and fungal specimens. The collections are worldwide in scope, with a focus on the state of Oregon and the Pacific Northwest.","logo":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/collicon/osc.jpg","url":"http://oregonstate.edu/dept/botany/herbarium/","emllink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=12","archivelink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/dwca/osc_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"spatafoj@science.oregonstate.edu"},{"last_name":"Joey Spatafora","email":"spatafoj@science.oregonstate.edu"},{"first_name":"Jessie","last_name":"Uehling","email":"jessie.uehling@oregonstate.edu"},{"last_name":"Joey Spatafora","email":"spatafoj@science.oregonstate.edu"},{"first_name":"Jessie","last_name":"Uehling","email":"jessie.uehling@oregonstate.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"e9c875f0-d9c9-426b-bdc8-0999eb1b6deb","totalCount":71005,"hitRatio":0.000014083515245405253},{"uuid":"7a31ab80-1cc0-4456-9dea-61c2e9031a6f","itemCount":1,"name":"Chrysler Herbarium - Mycological Collection","description":"The Chrysler Herbarium (CHRB) is the last internationally recognized herbarium still in existence in the state of New Jersey. Over 150,000 vascular plant and algal collections, about 7,000 moss and liverwort specimens, and 2,600 lichen specimens form are collection and are arranged and catalogued systematically. The collection is worldwide in scope, with an emphasis on New Jersey and the Mid-Atlantic area, and contains specimens back to the mid-1800s.","logo":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/collicon/chrb.png","url":"http://herbarium.rutgers.edu/","emllink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=58","archivelink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/dwca/chrb_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"herbarium@sebs.rutgers.edu"},{"first_name":"Chrysler","last_name":"Herbarium","email":"herbarium@sebs.rutgers.edu"},{"first_name":"James","last_name":"White","role":"Director","email":"james.f.white@rutgers.edu"},{"first_name":"Megan R.","last_name":"King","role":"Collections Manager","email":"megan.king@rutgers.edu"},{"first_name":"Chrysler","last_name":"Herbarium","email":"herbarium@sebs.rutgers.edu"},{"first_name":"James","last_name":"White","role":"Director","email":"james.f.white@rutgers.edu"},{"first_name":"Megan R.","last_name":"King","role":"Collections Manager","email":"megan.king@rutgers.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC3 BY-NC","publisher":"e9c875f0-d9c9-426b-bdc8-0999eb1b6deb","totalCount":34942,"hitRatio":0.000028618854101081794},{"uuid":"91c5eec8-0cdc-4be2-9a99-a15ae5ec3edc","itemCount":1,"name":"Cornell University Plant Pathology Herbarium","description":"The Cornell Plant Pathology Herbarium (CUP) is a large research collection of preserved fungi and other organisms that cause plant diseases. CUP is the fourth largest mycological herbarium in North America. We hold about 400,000 fungus and plant disease specimens, including over 8000 type specimens. Our main geographic strength is northeastern North America, but we also hold important collections from the tropics including the Caribbean, Mexico, South America, Southeast Asia and Macaronesia. Our collections include many rare fungal exsiccati as well as many authors' herbaria (Atkinson, Durand, Fairman, Gremmen, Honey, Korf, Stewart, Welch). The CUP Photograph Collection supplements our specimens and comprises roughly 60,000 historical, scientific photographs of mushrooms, agricultural practices, plant diseases, and portraits of scientists.","logo":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/collicon/cup.jpg","url":"http://www.plantpath.cornell.edu/CUPpages/","emllink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=23","archivelink":"https://mycoportal.org/portal/content/dwca/cup_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"cup-herbarium@cornell.edu"},{"first_name":"Kathie","last_name":"Hodge","role":"Director of the Fungarium","email":"cup-herbarium@cornell.edu"},{"first_name":"Teresa","last_name":"Iturriaga","role":"Collection Manager","email":"cup-herbarium@cornell.edu"},{"first_name":"Kathie","last_name":"Hodge","role":"Director of the Fungarium","email":"cup-herbarium@cornell.edu"},{"first_name":"Teresa","last_name":"Iturriaga","role":"Collection Manager","email":"cup-herbarium@cornell.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"e9c875f0-d9c9-426b-bdc8-0999eb1b6deb","totalCount":182221,"hitRatio":0.000005487841686743021},{"uuid":"ee9d38ef-c1db-44de-b8f2-62acb7049370","itemCount":1,"name":"Bell Museum fungi","description":"Records of specimens at the University of Minnesota herbarium (MIN)","logo":"http://bellatlas.umn.edu/content/collicon/min-fungi.jpg","url":"https://www.bellmuseum.umn.edu/fungi/","emllink":"https://bellatlas.umn.edu:443/content/dwca/min-fungi_dwc-a.eml","archivelink":"https://bellatlas.umn.edu:443/content/dwca/min-fungi_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"_"},{"email":"_"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"28ad3bdd-c0be-4789-aef6-dd50be864463","totalCount":76193,"hitRatio":0.000013124565248776134}],"aggs":{"rs":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"e95396c4-1cac-4c9b-b461-5f21cd978fc6","doc_count":7},{"key":"36d35b23-113e-4633-90ec-19d265a3b5f6","doc_count":5},{"key":"8f3b62fb-56ec-49e8-9f8f-bb257348291f","doc_count":2},{"key":"0583609b-202f-40d0-8021-4c019635d4c9","doc_cou